# Tools the repo's own tooling shells out to inside the default devShell.
# Every entry is justified by a real call site. Tools that live outside that
# shell by design (gremlins in devShells.mutate, nix-update via `nix run`)
# are not listed. This file is data: one tool per line, `#` comments and blank
# lines ignored, no shebang and no executable bit.
#
# .ci/check-devshell-provides reads this file in both directions:
# - forward: every entry here must resolve on PATH inside the devShell;
# - reverse: every devShell package must be where one of these binaries
#   RESOLVES on PATH, or back a formatter .treefmt.nix enables.
#
# gofumpt, nixfmt and taplo are absent because of that second escape (rule 2);
# see .ci/check-devshell-provides for why.

# run-all-checks: go build, go vet, go test, `go run ./cmd/github-repos-audit validate`
go
# invoked by .ci/run-lint-checks
golangci-lint
# .ci/check-vulns scans with it; the `vuln` recipe prints its full report
govulncheck
# .ci/build-release writes the release's CycloneDX SBOM with it
cyclonedx-gomod
# .ci/release-version computes the next version and its changelog section
# with it
git-cliff
# .ci/release-publish signs a release's SHA256SUMS with `cosign sign-blob`
# and checks the signature with `cosign verify-blob`
cosign
# .ci/check-vulns reads govulncheck's JSON report with it;
# .ci/check-breaking-check-list reads the schema's check list with it;
# .ci/build-release writes the release version into the SBOM with it; the
# stub gh of .ci/test-release-pull-request, .ci/test-refresh-pull-request and
# .ci/test-release-publish runs the script's own --jq filter with it, and
# .ci/test-release-publish edits the stub's answers with it
jq
# .ci/run-lint-checks (verify) and .ci/run-fixers (write); treefmt drives it too
shfmt
# the end-to-end test shells out to `prettier --check` on the golden README;
# .ci/release-pull-request formats the CHANGELOG.md it commits with it, and
# .ci/test-release-version checks generated sections with it
prettier
# .ci/run-lint-checks lints every tracked shell script
shellcheck
# invoked by .ci/run-lint-checks
yamllint
# invoked by .ci/run-lint-checks
actionlint
# invoked by .ci/run-lint-checks
zizmor
# invoked by .ci/run-lint-checks and .ci/run-fixers; .ci/test-release-version
# lints a generated changelog section with it
markdownlint-cli2
# .ci/run-lint-checks: every tracked file no formatter owns
editorconfig-checker
# .ci/run-lint-checks: the links every tracked Markdown file outside testdata
# carries
lychee
# invoked by .ci/run-lint-checks and .ci/run-fixers
typos
# invoked by .ci/run-lint-checks (whole-tree scan of the tracked files)
gitleaks
# .ci/run-lint-checks: audit.schema.json against its metaschema, then the
# golden audit.json fixtures against audit.schema.json
check-jsonschema
# invoked by .ci/run-lint-checks
statix
# invoked by .ci/run-lint-checks
deadnix
# invoked by .ci/run-lint-checks; the binary name, not its `renovate` package
renovate-config-validator
# every script but .ci/fetch-release-binary (which derives its root from its
# own path and calls no git) uses git: most derive the repo root via
# `git rev-parse`; check-account-data runs git grep and git rev-list;
# .ci/release-version reads tags and merge commits, .ci/release-pull-request
# resolves HEAD and .ci/release-publish looks for a release's tag and its
# release commit; audit.yml runs .ci/refresh-pull-request on a bare runner
# outside the devshell, where the runner's own git serves it
git
# internal/github shells out to `gh auth token` when GITHUB_TOKEN is unset;
# every .ci/*-pull-request script, .ci/release-pull-request included, commits
# through `gh api` and opens or updates its pull request with `gh pr`;
# .ci/release-publish asks whether a release or its tag exists, drafts,
# reads back and publishes the release with `gh release`, and reads the tag
# and the default branch's changelog with `gh api`;
# .ci/fetch-release-binary lists a commit's tags with `gh api`, downloads
# a release with `gh release download` and checks its provenance with
# `gh attestation verify`; it runs on a bare runner outside the devshell, so
# this entry covers only its test, and so does audit.yml's run of
# .ci/refresh-pull-request
gh
# the task runner .justfile documents; .ci/run-lint-checks runs `just --fmt`
# and .ci/check-readme-recipes runs `just --summary`
just
# invoked by .githooks/commit-msg
commitlint
# the gate's own nix; flake.nix pins it as nix.out
nix

# The baseline userland the gates shell out to. Declared so it is a dependency
# rather than an inherited nixpkgs internal.
# .ci/run-lint-checks copies the tracked tree for gitleaks with `cp --parents`
cp
# .ci/run-lint-checks creates the gitleaks scan directory with it;
# .ci/release-pull-request stages the changelog and pull request body in
# one, .ci/release-publish the release notes
mktemp
# .ci/refresh-pull-request, .ci/fuzz-pull-request and
# .ci/release-pull-request encode the files they commit for
# createCommitOnBranch; audit.yml runs .ci/refresh-pull-request on a bare
# runner outside the devshell, so this entry covers only its test
base64
# .ci/run-lint-checks feeds the tracked file list to cp through xargs
xargs
# .ci/check-coverage, .ci/check-patch-coverage and .ci/check-coverage-drop
# parse profiles, diffs and baselines with it; POSIX awk only, no gawk-isms
awk
# .ci/check-patch-coverage orders its findings by file, then line;
# .ci/build-release orders SHA256SUMS with it and .ci/release-publish the
# file names it compares
sort
# .ci/release-publish waits with it between two reads of the tag a publish
# created
sleep
# .ci/build-release writes SHA256SUMS with it; .ci/release-publish holds the
# files to that list before it uploads them; .ci/fetch-release-binary checks
# a downloaded binary against the list (on a bare runner, outside the
# devshell, so this entry covers only its test)
sha256sum
# .ci/check-patch-coverage drops its sort keys before printing
cut
# .ci/check-patch-coverage turns git grep -z's NUL separators into bytes awk
# can split on
tr
# .ci/check-account-data matches its patterns with `grep --extended-regexp`
# and `grep --fixed-strings`; .ci/check-patch-coverage's test greps its own
# findings, and .ci/test-release-publish the README for the heading the
# release notes link to. GNU long options, so gnugrep, not a BSD grep.
grep
# .ci/check-account-data strips allowed occurrences with `sed --regexp-extended`
# and escapes names for the pattern file; .ci/gremlins-pull-request reads the
# bumped version with `sed -n`
sed
